  ---
  Zeabur service config (summary)

  yagent-backend (Node service, always-on):
  - Build: npm install && npm run build · Start: npm start
  - Env: OPENAI_API_KEY, OPENAI_BASE_URL (if OpenRouter), OPENAI_MODEL, DISCORD_TOKEN, ENABLE_WEB=true, WEB_ORIGIN=https://<your-webui-domain>,
  WORKSPACE_DIR=/data, optional VOICE_API_KEY/VOICE_API_URL. (PORT auto.)
  - Env: OPENAI_API_KEY, OPENAI_BASE_URL (if OpenRouter), OPENAI_MODEL, DISCORD_TOKEN, ENABLE_WEB=true, WEB_ORIGIN=https://<your-webui-domain>,
  WORKSPACE_DIR=/data, optional VOICE_API_KEY/VOICE_API_URL. (PORT auto.)
  - Volume mounted at /data.

  yagent-webui (static site):

  You're going from a monorepo (yagent/ = backend src/ + web/) to two repos. Make each repo's build root the package root Zeabur detects:

  - yagent-backend ← contents of yagent/ minus web/ (root has the backend package.json, src/, skills/, plugins/, tsconfig).
  - yagent-webui ← contents of yagent/web/ at the repo root (so package.json, vite.config.ts, index.html, src/ are top-level).

  Watch-out: web/src/types.ts is hand-synced with backend src/events.ts (there's a comment saying so). After the split that stays a manual
  mirror — keep them in step when you change the event shape.

  ---
  Zeabur service config (summary)

  yagent-backend (Node service, always-on):
  - Build: npm install && npm run build · Start: npm start
  - Env: OPENAI_API_KEY, OPENAI_BASE_URL (if OpenRouter), OPENAI_MODEL, DISCORD_TOKEN, ENABLE_WEB=true, WEB_ORIGIN=https://<your-webui-domain>,
  WORKSPACE_DIR=/data, optional VOICE_API_KEY/VOICE_API_URL. (PORT auto.)
  - Volume mounted at /data.

  yagent-webui (static site):
  - Zeabur auto-detects Vite → build npm run build, serve dist/.
  - Build env: VITE_API_BASE=https://<your-backend-domain>.

  Verify: open the webui domain → indicator goes live (WSS connected), sessions seed in (CORS GET works); send a message → timeline streams
  (turn:start→tools→turn:end); DM the Discord bot → it replies and that session also appears in the web UI.